The Merry Outlaw of the Glade

♫2360978177

Old Sherwood was green and the morning was bright,  
When a fellow named Robin stepped into the light.  
The sheriff of Nottingham threw a big fit,  
For this rogue was too clever, too fast, and too quick.  
He wore Lincoln green from his toes to his head,  
And pooped on the laws that the monarchy bred.

He met Little John on a rickety bridge,  
And cracked him right over his oversized ridge.  
“You’re twice my own size!” Robin yelled with a grin,  
As he fell in the river right up to his chin.  
They laughed at the splash and they shook on the spot,  
And added the giant to their merry lot.

Then Friar Tuck joined them, a man of the cloth,  
Who loved a good turkey and gallons of broth.  
He’d pray for your soul while he emptied your purse,  
And hit you with sticks while repeating a verse.  
They lived in the forest, completely untamed,  
The jolliest bandits that ever were named!

