The Sheriff was greedy, a pompous old pig,  
Who sat in his castle and wore a bad wig.  
He taxed the poor peasants for walking the street,  
And stole the last boots from their blistering feet.  
So Robin would wait in the shadow of trees,  
To bring the fat nobles right down to their knees.

“Hand over your gold!” was the outlaw’s big cry,  
“To dress up the poor and to buy them some pie!”  
He’d rob the fat bishops of silver and plate,  
And pass it to beggars who stood at the gate.  
He’d strip the rich barons right down to their shorts,  
And mock their high status with cheeky retorts.

The Sheriff threw tournaments, setting a snare,  
With a bright golden arrow to draw Robin there.  
Our hero showed up in a sketchy disguise,  
With a patch on his cheek and some spit in his eyes.  
He split the grand bullseye right clean through the wood,  
And escaped with the prize, yelling: “Robin the Good!”

× × ×

He wooed Maid Marian under the moon,  
And hummed to the deer a ridiculous tune.  
He lived by his bow and his absolute cheek,  
The nightmare of barons, the friend of the weak.  
And when he grew old and his energy passed,  
He shot one last arrow to mark where he’d rest—  
A jolly old scoundrel, the king of the thieves,  
Who still takes a bow through the green Sherwood leaves!
